      "writtenOn": "2026-04-02T19:19:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just thinking: maybe WARN_ON or WARN_ON_ONCE would be more appropriate for this?\n\nAlthough, currently in non-debug mode WARN_ON just does nothing, so maybe BUG_ON is better. Although BUG_ON in non-debug mode currently doesn\u0027t provide any useful diagnostics either, it just hangs in HLT, but we may change that  ( @jaszczyk@google.com  is currently looking into how we could improve that). And hanging in HLT it still better than this infinite spin-waiting at least in terms of power consumption. :)",
      "revId": "a56ebb58bd5815a36a2a942bc0fb30addaaeba8e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d530986d_a6625856",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 2131,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-03T01:34:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "OK, let me try to understand this. If we plan to improve the WARN/BUG behavior, I guess they will be similar to the linux kernel\u0027s usage? Then, WARN_ON/WARN_ON_ONCE will just print a warning log in the debug mode and then continue by skipping the warn instruction, so we are still in the infinite spin loop for the debug node. And for non-debug mode, WARN_ON/WARN_ON_ONCE will still do nothing? If so, it means we prefer to just print some warning log and continue the spin loop rather than hang the system, right?",
      "parentUuid": "ae358245_71e4d9b8",
      "revId": "a56ebb58bd5815a36a2a942bc0fb30addaaeba8e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "48a57d42_8ae7ecea",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 2131,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-03T02:45:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For non-debug mode (as that is the mode used in production) we\u0027d like to print at least some diagnostics (instead of just silently hanging) when the hypervisor crashes or BUGs. For example, in pKVM-ARM in non-debug mode, currently, when the hypervisor crashes or BUGs, it injects some exception into the host so that the host kernel panics with a call trace which doesn\u0027t really contain the hypervisor stack, but at least contains an indication that it is the pKVM hypervisor that crashed. (Greg might tell more details.)\n\n\u003e it means we prefer to just print some warning log and continue the spin loop rather than hang the system, right?\n\nIf we just print a warning and if it means we only do that in debug mode, it means in production builds we just continue spinning without printing anything (and if we continue spinning, most likely we will spin forever, i.e. the system is already hung)... So I\u0027m not really sure which option is more preferable.\n\nBTW actually I\u0027d probably be fine if we just had a simple `while (READ_ONCE(vcpu-\u003emode) \u003d\u003d EXITING_GUEST_MODE) cpu_relax();` loop here, without counting iterations and TSC cycles (i.e. without trying to provide any diagnostics in this hypothetical situation). Maybe I\u0027d even prefer that (for code simplicity).",
      "parentUuid": "d530986d_a6625856",
      "revId": "a56ebb58bd5815a36a2a942bc0fb30addaaeba8e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f83f2437_264e4c02",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 2131,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-03T05:13:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e For non-debug mode (as that is the mode used in production) we\u0027d like to print at least some diagnostics (instead of just silently hanging) when the hypervisor crashes or BUGs. For example, in pKVM-ARM in non-debug mode, currently, when the hypervisor crashes or BUGs, it injects some exception into the host so that the host kernel panics with a call trace which doesn\u0027t really contain the hypervisor stack, but at least contains an indication that it is the pKVM hypervisor that crashed. (Greg might tell more details.)\n\nSo for WARN, the pKVM will inject some exception to the host to print some warning messages, meanwhile not hung and continue to run. And the host will also not hung itself during handling this exception, right?\n\nAnd for BUG, the pKVM will also inject some exception to the host to print some bug message, meanwhile the pKVM will hung itself on this CPU via hlt?\n\n\u003e If we just print a warning and if it means we only do that in debug mode, it means in production builds we just continue spinning without printing anything (and if we continue spinning, most likely we will spin forever, i.e. the system is already hung)... So I\u0027m not really sure which option is more preferable.\n\nIf so, the only difference of using WARN and BUG is whether the CPU will be hlt or continue spin. From the function point of view, either case will stop this CPU. But WARN will give the CPU a 2nd chance to break the spin, BUG will save more power but if this CPU is stopped, I think I will not care about the power consumption in this scenario and want to reboot immediately...\n\nSo maybe a warn_on_once is better?\n\n\u003e BTW actually I\u0027d probably be fine if we just had a simple while (READ_ONCE(vcpu-\u003emode) \u003d\u003d EXITING_GUEST_MODE) cpu_relax(); loop here, without counting iterations and TSC cycles (i.e. without trying to provide any diagnostics in this hypothetical situation). Maybe I\u0027d even prefer that (for code simplicity).\n\nIf we will have some improved BUG/WARN for the debug mode to show something, maybe it is worth to have this tsc check so that we will know if this infinite spin loop causes any issue or not?",
      "parentUuid": "48a57d42_8ae7ecea",
      "revId": "a56ebb58bd5815a36a2a942bc0fb30addaaeba8e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "efb52b5d_2bd119e4",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 2131,
      "author": {
        "id": 3484945
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-03T08:00:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just FYI, Yes I am working and experimenting with improving BUG and WARN handling for both production (without DEBUG mode) and non-production (with DEBUG) modes. I already have some WIP patches and will share this improved BUG and WARN handling soon, probably next week.\n\nAnd to give more context: I\u0027ve started with mimic what pKVM-ARM is doing which is delegating printing warring/bug messages to host via injecting nmi and registering panic handler, which handles logging, on the host side and have it already working.\n\nNevertheless I\u0027ve also started experimenting with a different approach especially for BUG (and other fatal errors in hyp such as null pointer dereference) in prod mode and actually do not delegate anything to host since hyp is in undefined state already during such handling so I think it is safer to just halt all other cpus and write diagnostic to ramoops directly from the hyp and reset (no return to host). For WARN_ON it is different and we can continue execution and delegate printing to host.\n\nI have both approach: 1) delegating printing to host via nmi and 2) logging errors directly from hyp to ramoops (without delegating to host) WIP implemented and working - will share them soon.\n\n\u003e If we will have some improved BUG/WARN for the debug mode to show something, maybe it is worth to have this tsc check so that we will know if this infinite spin loop causes any issue or not?\n\nI agree I think having BUG/WARNING here will be better option - if we use BUG it will log error and reset the system, if WARN - it will log error and continue running (in production mode with new improvements for WARN/BUG handling). 1s is a lot so I think it is rather BUG(), right?",
      "parentUuid": "f83f2437_264e4c02",
      "revId": "a56ebb58bd5815a36a2a942bc0fb30addaaeba8e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7288d089_e3b98ec1",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
