      },
      "lineNbr": 2131,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-03T08:26:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e 1) delegating printing to host via nmi \n\nIf my understanding is correct, in this case, the pKVM will also continue to run until return to the host to handle the NMI for printing bug message and triggering panic?\n\nThe current code is implemented based on the assumption that once BUG happens, the pKVM will stop and not continue to run, e.g.,\n```\n    BUG_ON(!is_memory_range(phys, size));\n    set_host_mem_pgstate(phys, size, PKVM_PAGE_OWNED, PKVM_ID_HOST);\n```\n\nIf we continue after `BUG_ON(!is_memory_range(phys, size));`, `set_host_mem_pgstate(phys, size, PKVM_PAGE_OWNED, PKVM_ID_HOST);` may trigger a page fault exception which still hlt in the pKVM, or even worse modify some memory expected and silently continue with potential security issue.\n\nIf the above understanding is correct, I think we have to go with approach 2) unless we check each place of the BUG and make it safely continue?\n\n\u003e I agree I think having BUG/WARNING here will be better option - if we use BUG it will log error and reset the system, if WARN - it will log error and continue running (in production mode with new improvements for WARN/BUG handling). 1s is a lot so I think it is rather BUG(), right?\n\nmakes sense, especially the BUG in approach 2) can reset the system.",
      "parentUuid": "efb52b5d_2bd119e4",
      "revId": "a56ebb58bd5815a36a2a942bc0fb30addaaeba8e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "efc2bca2_222ddee4",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 2131,
      "author": {
        "id": 3484945
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-03T09:06:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In general I think that 2) approach is a way to go mainly because it prevents to returning to untrusted Host VM, during such fatal errors and allow host to react on bugs in hyp which isn\u0027t safe I think.\n\nBut regarding above comment for above 1) NMI like solution, I think it could be done in a way that hyp will not continue after BUG_ON since BUG_ON results with u2d and triggers default_exception_handler(). In this handler hyp after preparing everything for the host needed for logging this wrong behavior in my WIP patches I\u0027ve used GUEST_INTR_STATE_NMI, followed by NMI injection and vmresume which AFAIU should force host VM to take over and handle nmi and panic (but since host is untrusted it could start inspecting and use maliciously this nondeterministic state instead of logging and panicing).\n\nBut again I think that 2) approach with logging errors directly from hyp to ramoops is a better (with 1) e.g. malicious host could overwrite its nmi handler to try to \u0027inspect\u0027 the buggy hypervisor which is not possible with 2)).",
      "parentUuid": "7288d089_e3b98ec1",
      "revId": "a56ebb58bd5815a36a2a942bc0fb30addaaeba8e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
